#import "../bookmaster/bookmaster.typ": *
#import "msg-lib.typ": msg

#msg("OFTP201W", "name CALLED FROM address, NOT address", [A caller gave the Odette ID of partner #var("name"), but the call came from the first address, and the partner's entry in #cmd("OFTPPTN") names the second one in #cmd("ADDRESS="). OFTPD does not accept the caller. The session ends with reason 03 (user code not known), and an #cmd("OFTP290W") line "unknown caller" with the Odette ID follows.], [Check the #cmd("ADDRESS=") value of the partner. If the partner's address has changed, correct #cmd("OFTPPTN") and enter #cmd("F OFTPD,REFRESH"). If the partner should not be calling from that address, nothing is needed.])

#msg("OFTP202W", "name REFUSED, n SESSIONS RUNNING, SESSIONS=n", [A caller identified itself as partner #var("name"), but the partner already has as many sessions as its #cmd("SESSIONS=") value allows. The first number is the sessions now running (calls OFTPD has under way count too), the second the limit. OFTPD ends the new session with reason 08 (resource not available, try later), which asks the partner to try later. An #cmd("OFTP290P") line "caller id refused, reason 08" follows.], [None if it happens now and then. If it is regular, raise #cmd("SESSIONS=") for the partner in #cmd("OFTPPTN") (then #cmd("F OFTPD,REFRESH")), or check why the partner opens more sessions than agreed.])

#msg("OFTP203P", "name: SESSION ENDED, ESID nn text, FILES n OUT n IN", [A session with partner #var("name") has ended. #var("nn") is the end-of-session reason (ESID) code, with 00 for a normal end. The codes OFTPD sends itself are 01 command not recognised, 02 protocol violation, 03 user code not known, 04 invalid password, 06 command with invalid data, 07 buffer size mismatch, 08 resources not available (try later), 09 time out, 10 mode or capabilities not compatible, 12 secure authentication not possible, and 99 unspecified (also a lost connection or a failed send). A partner may send others. The text is #cmd("(OURS)") if OFTPD sent the ESID and #cmd("(PARTNER)") if the partner sent it. The last two numbers are the files sent (OUT) and received (IN) in this session. Variant: #cmd("name: SESSION ENDED BY ABEND code, FILES n OUT n IN") when the session ended by an abend. #cmd("OFTP209E") was written before it. Before the partner is known, the caller's address stands in place of the name. If OFTPD had called the partner and the session ended with a code other than 00 before any file was transferred, or by an abend, the next call waits for the #cmd("RETRY=") interval. If the partner ended it with reason 08, the wait is 5 to 60 seconds (see #cmd("OFTP218P")).], [None. Information only. A reason other than 00 is explained by the #cmd("OFTP290W") and #cmd("OFTP290P") lines written just before this one.])

#msg("OFTP204W", "address: CALL REFUSED, n SESSIONS", [A caller from #var("address") is refused: #var("n") sessions already run, which is the #cmd("MAXSESS=") value of #cmd("OFTPPRM") or more. OFTPD closes the connection at once and does not read the caller's Odette ID.], [If it recurs, raise #cmd("MAXSESS=") in #cmd("OFTPPRM") and restart OFTPD. Use #cmd("F OFTPD,D S") to see the number of sessions now running.])

#msg("OFTP205P", "address: CALL RECEIVED", [OFTPD accepted a connection from #var("address") and starts a session for it. The partner is not known yet. The session's later messages show its name.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP206P", "name: CALLING AT host PORT n", [OFTPD calls partner #var("name") at the #cmd("HOST=") and #cmd("PORT=") of its entry in #cmd("OFTPPTN"). It does so because a file waits for the partner, or because an operator or user asked for a call (#cmd("F OFTPD,CALL name") or the #cmd("CALL") command of the utility). OFTPD waits about 5 seconds for the connection.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP207P", "name: NOT REACHED", [The connection to partner #var("name") could not be made within about 5 seconds, or its host name could not be resolved. Each file due for the partner counts this as a failed attempt (unless another session with the partner is running), and the file waits #cmd("RETRY=") seconds times the number of attempts so far (at most one day). The partner is not called again by OFTPD before the #cmd("RETRY=") interval has passed, but #cmd("F OFTPD,CALL name") calls it at once. This line repeats with every call. When a file has used up its attempts (#cmd("MAXRETRY=")), #cmd("OFTP215W") is written, or #cmd("OFTP242W") for a relayed file.], [Check that the partner is running and that #cmd("HOST=") and #cmd("PORT=") in #cmd("OFTPPTN") are right. #cmd("OFTP215W") is the message that asks for action.])

#msg("OFTP208W", "name: SESSION WITHOUT ABEND RECOVERY, RC n", [OFTPD could not set up abend recovery for the session with #var("name") (return code #var("n")). The session runs all the same, but unprotected: an abend in it is not caught, and the session would stay counted as running. Before the partner is known, the caller's address stands in place of the name.], [None at the console. If it recurs, note the return code and report it.])

#msg("OFTP209E", "name: SESSION ABEND code, CLEANING UP", [The session with #var("name") ended by an abend. #var("code") is a system abend (S followed by three hexadecimal digits) or a user abend (U followed by four digits). OFTPD releases what the session held, and then handles its files: a file being sent is counted as a failed attempt (it is queued again, or held if its attempts are used up, see #cmd("OFTP215W"), or given up if it is a relayed file, see #cmd("OFTP242W")), and a file being received is discarded. #cmd("OFTP203P") with ENDED BY ABEND follows. OFTPD itself goes on. If it had called the partner, it waits for the #cmd("RETRY=") interval before the next call.], [Look at the abend code and at the log lines before this one. Check the transfers of the partner with #cmd("F OFTPD,D Q") or the #cmd("LIST") command of the utility.])

#msg("OFTP210E", "name: key data set CANNOT BE OPENED", [OFTPD took the file with spool key #var("key") for partner #var("name") to send, but could not open the data set. For a member the name shows as #cmd("dsn(member)"). The transfer is set to FAILED with reason 12 and the text "data set cannot be opened". It is not retried by itself. OFTPD goes on with the next file in the session.], [Check that the data set exists, is not in use by another job and can be read. Then queue the file again with the #cmd("RETRY") command of the utility (#cmd("RETRY key")), or remove the transfer with #cmd("DELETE").])

#msg("OFTP211P", "name: key dsn SENT, n RECORDS", [Partner #var("name") accepted the file with spool key #var("key"), sent as virtual file #var("dsn"), after the last record. #var("n") is the number of records read from the data set. The transfer is in the state SENT until the partner's receipt (EERP) arrives. #cmd("OFTP213P") is written then.], [None. Information only. A file in the state SENT is not yet delivered.])

#msg("OFTP212P", "name: key dsn REFUSED, REASON nn RETRY c", [Partner #var("name") did not take the file with spool key #var("key"). #var("nn") is the reason code of the partner's refusal, taken from its start-file negative answer (SFNA) or end-file negative answer (EFNA). OFTPD itself uses 99 if the file cannot be described to the partner, and 12 on a read error. #var("c") is Y if the refusal can be retried, and N if not. A refusal of the end of a file (EFNA) is always N. With Y, the file waits for another attempt, #cmd("RETRY=") seconds times the number of attempts so far (at most one day). When #cmd("MAXRETRY=") attempts are used up, it is held and #cmd("OFTP215W") is written (for a relayed file #cmd("OFTP242W")). With N, the transfer is set to REJECTED and OFTPD does not try again.], [For N, find the reason in the partner's documentation or ask the partner, correct the cause and queue the file again with #cmd("RETRY key") of the utility. For Y, none.])

#msg("OFTP213P", "name: key dsn DELIVERED", [Partner #var("name") sent a positive receipt (EERP) for the file with spool key #var("key"), virtual file #var("dsn"). The partner has the file. The transfer is DELIVERED. For a relayed file the response is passed on to the partner the file came from (#cmd("OFTP240P")). If it was queued with #cmd("DISP=DELETE"), the data set is deleted now. See #cmd("OFTP215P") and #cmd("OFTP216W"). Variant: #cmd("name: key dsn NOT DELIVERED (NERP)") when the partner sent a negative receipt (NERP): the partner rejected the file. The transfer is set to REJECTED with the reason of the NERP, and the data set is kept.], [None for DELIVERED. For NOT DELIVERED, show the transfer with #cmd("SHOW key") of the utility to see the reason, ask the partner, and send again with #cmd("RETRY key") if wanted.])

#msg("OFTP214P", "name: EERP FOR UNKNOWN FILE dsn", [Partner #var("name") sent a receipt for virtual file #var("dsn"), and OFTPD has no sent transfer for that partner that matches it (name, date and time of the file). OFTPD ignores the receipt. Variant: NERP in place of EERP for a negative receipt. A transfer that was deleted or purged (#cmd("KEEPDAYS=")) before the receipt came is one cause.], [None, if you know the transfer was deleted. Otherwise check the partner's records. Use #cmd("LIST") of the utility to see the transfers still in the spool.])

#msg("OFTP215P", "name: key data set DELETED (DISP=DELETE)", [The file with spool key #var("key") was queued with #cmd("DISP=DELETE"). The partner's positive receipt (EERP) has come in, and OFTPD has deleted the data set. The transfer stays in the spool as DELIVERED. This is written for the file sent, not for a file passed on for another partner.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP215W", "name: key dsn HELD AFTER n ATTEMPTS", [The file with spool key #var("key") for partner #var("name") failed #var("n") times, which is the #cmd("MAXRETRY=") value of #cmd("OFTPPRM"). The attempts failed because the partner was not reached, the partner refused the file with a reason that allows a retry, or the session ended during the transfer. OFTPD does not try again. The transfer is HELD. It stays in the spool, and a person decides what happens to it. With #cmd("MAXRETRY=0") no file is ever held. A relayed file is not held but given up (#cmd("OFTP242W")).], [Find the cause (see the lines before it for this partner). Then free the file with #cmd("RELEASE key") of the utility, or #cmd("F OFTPD,RELEASE name") for all held files of the partner, which starts the attempts again from zero. Or remove it with #cmd("DELETE key").])

#msg("OFTP216E", "name: ABEND code CLOSING THE FILES OF THE SESSION", [The session with #var("name") had files open when it ended, and an abend occurred while OFTPD closed them. #var("code") has the same form as in #cmd("OFTP209E"). OFTPD does not close a file twice, and goes on. What the cleanup had not done yet is left undone: the spool record of a file being sent may stay ACTIVE, and a file being received may leave its spool entry and temporary data set behind.], [Check the transfers of the partner with #cmd("LIST") of the utility. A transfer that is still ACTIVE after the session has ended cannot be changed with the utility. When OFTPD is next started, an ACTIVE send is set to WAITING again, and an ACTIVE receive is discarded with its temporary data set.])

#msg("OFTP216W", "name: key data set NOT DELETED (DISP=DELETE)", [The file with spool key #var("key") was queued with #cmd("DISP=DELETE"), and the partner's positive receipt (EERP) has come in. OFTPD tried to delete the data set and failed. The transfer is DELIVERED, with the text that the data set was NOT deleted. The data set is still there.], [Delete the data set yourself if it is no longer needed. Check whether it is in use, or was deleted before.])

#msg("OFTP217W", "name: NO ROOM FOR ANOTHER SESSION SUBTASK", [OFTPD has no free place to keep one more session task. It closes the connection at once and starts no session. #var("name") is the partner OFTPD was calling. For an incoming call, whose partner is not known yet, the caller's address stands in its place. A call that OFTPD made is tried again at the next round of its scheduler. The limit is 32 tasks for each of the two parts of OFTPD that start sessions, twice the largest #cmd("MAXSESS=") value. The message is therefore not expected while #cmd("MAXSESS=") is respected.], [If it recurs, report it. Use #cmd("F OFTPD,D S") to see the sessions that run.])

#msg("OFTP218P", "name BUSY, CALLED AGAIN IN n S", [OFTPD called partner #var("name"), and the partner ended the session with reason 08 (resource not available, try later) before any file was transferred. The partner is most likely in a session with OFTPD already, perhaps one it started itself. OFTPD calls the partner again after a pause of #var("n") seconds, a value between 5 and 60 chosen at random. The pause is deliberately different from the partner's own, so that two monitors that call each other do not collide again.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP219E", "SPOOL REQUEST n ABEND code, ANSWERED WITH AN ERROR", [A request to the spool ended in an abend, which OFTPD caught. #var("code") is a system abend (S and three hexadecimal digits) or a user abend (U and four digits). #var("n") is the kind of request: 0 add, 1 read, 2 write, 3 write if the state is unchanged, 4 delete, 5 next, 6 purge. The request is answered with an error. The caller gets a spool error, and a utility command usually answers that the spool is not available. The spool task goes on with the next request.], [Note the abend code and look for the abend message or a dump on the console. If it recurs, stop and start OFTPD, and check the spool data set.], dest: "console only")

#msg("OFTP219W", "SPOOL THREAD WITHOUT ABEND RECOVERY", [The task that serves the spool could not set up abend recovery for a request. It carries out the request all the same. An abend in it would end the task, and every later request to the spool would wait for good. The message is written for each such request.], [Report it if it recurs.], dest: "console only")

#msg("OFTP220E", "name: dsn NOT RECEIVED, data set NOT ALLOCATED (nn)", [OFTPD could not create the data set for a file that partner #var("name") offers. #var("dsn") is the virtual file name of the file. The data set shown is the temporary one, named from #cmd("TMPPREFIX=") and the transfer key. #var("nn") is the reason that OFTPD returns to the partner in its refusal: 04 the record format is not one of F, V, U or T, 05 the record length is outside what OFTPD can store, 12 the allocation or the open failed, 13 a data set of that name exists already. The refusal says that the partner may try again later. The session goes on, and no spool entry is kept for the file.], [For 12 and 13 check the data set shown, and the #cmd("TMPPREFIX="), #cmd("UNIT=") and #cmd("VOLSER=") settings of #cmd("OFTPPRM"). Look for free space on the volume and for an old data set of that name. If a receive rule matched the file, its #cmd("UNIT="), #cmd("VOLSER=") and #cmd("SPACE=") count instead of the settings (see @rcv-kw). For 04 and 05 the file must be sent in another format, which is the partner's choice.])

#msg("OFTP221E", "name: dsn NOT RENAMED TO dsn", [A file from partner #var("name") arrived in full, but OFTPD could not rename the temporary data set (the first name) to its final name (the second). The cause is not given in the message. Unless the receive rule has #cmd("REPLACE=Y"), OFTPD checked before the transfer that the final name was free, so a data set of that name created in the meantime is one possible cause. With #cmd("REPLACE=Y"), the old data set is put aside first, and OFTPD renames it back (see #cmd("OFTP227E") if it cannot). OFTPD deletes the temporary data set and its spool entry. It answers the partner with a negative end-of-file response, reason 12, so the file counts as not delivered.], [Look in the catalog for a data set of the final name (built from #cmd("INPREFIX=") and the virtual file name, or by the receive rule), and look for system messages before this line. The partner has to send the file again.])

#msg("OFTP222P", "name: key dsn RECEIVED AS data set, n RECORDS", [OFTPD received a file from partner #var("name") and stored it. #var("dsn") is the virtual file name the partner used. The data set shown is where the file is, named from #cmd("INPREFIX=") and the virtual file name, or by the receive rule that matched the file. If a name from #cmd("INPREFIX=") is taken, the transfer key is added to it. #var("n") is the number of records written. OFTPD owes the partner an end-to-end response (EERP) for the file and sends it in this or the next session with the partner.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP223W", "name: dsn FOR id REFUSED, reason", [Partner #var("name") offers a file addressed to Odette ID #var("id"), and OFTPD refuses it. The refusal is returned to the partner with reason 02 (invalid destination) and the session goes on. The reason text is one of three. NOT OUR ODETTE ID: the file is not addressed to the ID of OFTPD (#cmd("ODETTEID=") in #cmd("OFTPPRM")) nor to the ID OFTPD uses towards this partner (#cmd("OURID=")), and relaying is off. NO ROUTE: relaying is on (#cmd("RELAY=Y")) but no enabled partner in #cmd("OFTPPTN") has that Odette ID, or the partner named by its #cmd("VIA=") is not defined. IT WOULD GO BACK: the way on to that ID leads to the partner that sent the file.], [Check the Odette ID with the sender. To relay files for a partner, set #cmd("RELAY=Y") in #cmd("OFTPPRM"), restart OFTPD, and define the final partner in #cmd("OFTPPTN"), with #cmd("VIA=") naming the partner it is reached through.])

#msg("OFTP224W", "name: dsn REFUSED, RULE rule MAKES NO VALID DATA SET NAME", [Partner #var("name") offers a file with the virtual file name #var("dsn"), and the data set name that receive rule #var("rule") makes for it is not valid: a qualifier is empty, starts with a digit, is longer than 8 characters, or the name is longer than 44 characters. A rule whose #cmd("DSN=") has #cmd("%CTR") makes an invalid name for a file whose time field has no counter. OFTPD refuses the file with the reason 01 (invalid file name) and tells the partner not to try again. Nothing is allocated, and no spool entry is kept.], [Look at the #cmd("DSN=") of the rule and at the virtual file name. Give the partner a name the rule can use, or change the rule (see @rcv-name), then #cmd("F OFTPD,REFRESH").])

#msg("OFTP225W", "name: dsn REFUSED, dsn EXISTS (RULE rule, REPLACE=N)", [Partner #var("name") offers a file with the virtual file name #var("dsn"). Receive rule #var("rule") names the data set for it, the data set shown after #cmd("REFUSED,") exists already, and the rule has #cmd("REPLACE=N"), which is the default. OFTPD refuses the file with the reason 13 (duplicate file) and tells the partner that it may try again later. Nothing is allocated, and no spool entry is kept.], [If the old data set is to be replaced, set #cmd("REPLACE=Y") in the rule and refresh. If not, delete or rename the old data set, or let the partner send the file under another name. A partner that tries again is refused again until then.])

#msg("OFTP226E", "name: dsn NOT RECEIVED, dsn CANNOT BE REPLACED, IT STAYS", [A file from partner #var("name") with the virtual file name #var("dsn") arrived in full, and the rule has #cmd("REPLACE=Y"), but the old data set of the final name could not be put aside: it is not a plain sequential data set (it has become a partitioned data set or a VSAM cluster since the start of the file), or its rename failed, for example because another job has it allocated. The old data set stays as it was. OFTPD deletes the temporary data set and the spool entry, and answers the partner with a negative end-of-file response, reason 12. The partner has to send the file again.], [Find out why the data set cannot be renamed, with the system messages before this line. When it can be, the partner sends the file again.])

#msg("OFTP227E", "name: THE OLD dsn IS NOW dsn, NOT RENAMED BACK", [Receive rule with #cmd("REPLACE=Y"): the old data set (the first name) was renamed to the backup name (the second, under #cmd("TMPPREFIX=")), and OFTPD could not give it its own name again. Two causes: the new file could not be renamed to the final name (then #cmd("OFTP221E") follows), or OFTPD found the backup of an interrupted transfer when it started and the rename back failed. The old data set is the one with the second name.], [Check whether the final name exists. If it does not, rename the backup (the second name) to the final name by hand. Delete the backup only when the final name exists and holds the file you want.])

#msg("OFTP228W", "name: THE OLD dsn IS NOW dsn, NOT DELETED", [Receive rule with #cmd("REPLACE=Y"): the new file is under the final name (the first name), and OFTPD could not delete the backup of the old one (the second name, under #cmd("TMPPREFIX=")). The transfer is complete and is acknowledged to the partner. #cmd("OFTP229I") is not written.], [Look at the backup. Delete it when you do not need it.])

#msg("OFTP229I", "name: dsn REPLACED", [Receive rule with #cmd("REPLACE=Y"): the old data set of the name shown has been replaced by the file from partner #var("name"). The backup of the old version has been deleted. #cmd("OFTP222P") follows for the same file.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP230P", "key QUEUED FOR name: data set AS dsn", [A user of the utility (the #cmd("SEND") command) queued a data set to send. #var("key") is the transfer key. #var("name") is the partner OFTPD will call or wait for. If the file is for a partner reached through another (#cmd("VIA=")), it is the partner of the session, not the final recipient. The data set is shown without a member name. #var("dsn") is the virtual file name under which the partner will know the file. The transfer is held, not sent, if the user gave #cmd("HOLD").], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP231P", "key DELETED BY user", [The transfer with the key #var("key") was deleted from the spool by user #var("user"), with the #cmd("DELETE") command of the utility. Only its spool entry goes. Data sets remain. A transfer that is being sent or received cannot be deleted.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP232P", "key verb BY user", [User #var("user") changed the state of the transfer with the key #var("key"). #var("verb") is HOLD (a queued or waiting send is held), RELEASE (a held send is queued, with its attempts counted from zero) or RETRY (a transfer that is not queued is queued again, also one that was sent already, which the partner then sees twice). OFTPD wakes the scheduler.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP233I", "n SENDS FOR name HELD|RELEASED BY OPERATOR", [Answer to #cmd("F OFTPD,HOLD name") or #cmd("F OFTPD,RELEASE name"). #var("n") is the number of sends changed, and may be 0. #var("name") is the operand as typed, so it can be #cmd("ALL"). #cmd("HOLD") changes the sends that are queued or waiting for a retry, so they are not called for. A send that is already in a session is not stopped. #cmd("RELEASE") changes the held sends back to queued, with the count of attempts set back to 0, and wakes the scheduler. A #cmd("HOLD") or #cmd("RELEASE") for a partner from the OFTP utility is logged as #cmd("OFTP233P").], [None. Information only. Use #cmd("F OFTPD,D Q") to see the state of the transfers.])

#msg("OFTP233P", "n SENDS FOR name state BY user", [User #var("user") held or released the sends for a partner with one #cmd("HOLD") or #cmd("RELEASE") command of the utility that names the partner and not a key. #var("n") is the number of sends changed. #var("name") is the partner, or an asterisk for all. If the command gave a virtual file name pattern, it follows the partner. #var("state") is HELD or RELEASED. Only queued and waiting sends are held, and only held ones released. A user who is not an administrator (#cmd("ADMIN=") in #cmd("OFTPPRM")) changes only their own transfers. A caller without a userid owns none, so the user is shown as A CALLER WITHOUT USERID and #var("n") is 0.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP234W", "name: dsn REFUSED, dsn IS NOT A SEQUENTIAL DATA SET AND IS NOT REPLACED (RULE rule)", [Partner #var("name") offers a file with the virtual file name #var("dsn"). Receive rule #var("rule") has #cmd("REPLACE=Y"), and the data set it names (the second name) exists but is not a plain sequential data set. It is a partitioned data set or a VSAM cluster. OFTPD does not replace those. It refuses the file with the reason 12 (access method failure) and tells the partner not to try again. The data set is untouched.], [Change the #cmd("DSN=") of the rule so that it names a sequential data set, or remove the data set.])

#msg("OFTP235I", "n FINISHED TRANSFERS OLDER THAN n DAYS DELETED BY who", [OFTPD has deleted finished transfers from the spool. A transfer is finished when it is delivered, confirmed, rejected or failed, and it is deleted when its last change is older than the number of days. #var("who") is #cmd("OPERATOR") for #cmd("F OFTPD,PURGE"), the user ID of the caller for the #cmd("PURGE") command of the OFTP utility (#cmd("A CALLER WITHOUT USERID") if there is none), or #cmd("KEEPDAYS") for the housekeeping that OFTPD does at start and then once a day if #cmd("KEEPDAYS") in #cmd("OFTPPRM") is greater than 0. The daily housekeeping writes the message only if it deleted something. The other two always write it, also for 0 transfers.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP236E", "SPOOL HOUSEKEEPING FAILED AFTER n, BY who", [The deletion of finished transfers stopped because an access to the spool failed. #var("n") is the number of transfers deleted before the failure. #var("who") is as in #cmd("OFTP235I"). The transfers not yet reached remain in the spool. The daily housekeeping tries again after one day.], [Check the spool data set. #cmd("OFTP219E") on the console reports an abend of a spool request. Repeat the command after the cause is removed.])

#msg("OFTP237E", "verb OF name: SPOOL NOT AVAILABLE", [The command #cmd("F OFTPD,HOLD") or #cmd("F OFTPD,RELEASE") could not complete because an access to the spool failed. #var("verb") is #cmd("HOLD") or #cmd("RELEASE"), and #var("name") is the operand as typed. Some sends may have been changed before the failure.], [Use #cmd("F OFTPD,D Q") to see which transfers changed. Check the spool data set. #cmd("OFTP219E") on the console reports an abend of a spool request. Repeat the command after the cause is removed.])

#msg("OFTP238W", "SEND OF dsn REFUSED: reason", [The #cmd("SEND") command of the utility named data set #var("dsn"), and the security system does not allow the caller to read it (to delete it, if #cmd("DISP(DELETE)") is given). The check is made only if a security product is installed. The send is not queued, and the utility reports the refusal to the caller. The reason is one of: "the caller has no userid", "no security environment for name (RACINIT rc n)", or "name may not read dsn" (or "delete" for #cmd("DISP(DELETE)")), where name is the caller's userid and dsn the data set without a member name. The check is made for the caller and not for OFTPD, which reads the file later.], [Give the user the access to the data set, or let a user who has it queue the send.])

#msg("OFTP239I", "name: THE OLD dsn PUT BACK FROM dsn", [At start OFTPD found a receive that a stop had interrupted. The rule had #cmd("REPLACE=Y"), and the stop came after the old data set was renamed to its backup name (the second name) and before the new file was renamed to the final name. The final name did not exist. OFTPD has renamed the backup to the final name, so the old data set is back. The partial receive is discarded, and the partner has to send the file again. #cmd("OFTP040P") follows.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP240P", "name: key RELAY: type FOR dsn QUEUED", [A response for a relayed file is queued for partner #var("name"), the partner the file came from. #var("type") is EERP when the final recipient confirmed the file, and NERP when it, or a later partner, refused it or OFTPD gave up sending it on. #var("dsn") is the virtual file name. #var("key") is the transfer key of the queued response. OFTPD sends it when it next talks to the partner.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP241P", "name: key RELAY: dsn FROM id FOR id QUEUED FOR name", [OFTPD received a file that is for another Odette ID and queues it for the way on. The first name is the partner that sent it, the first ID is the originator, the second ID the final recipient, and the last name is the partner OFTPD sends it to. That is the final recipient itself, or the partner it is reached through (#cmd("VIA=")). The file is stored under a temporary name and is not changed. OFTPD sends no response of its own, because only the final recipient does that.], [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP242W", "name: key RELAY: dsn FOR id GIVEN UP AFTER n ATTEMPTS", [OFTPD could not send a relayed file to partner #var("name") in #var("n") attempts. That is the #cmd("MAXRETRY=") limit of #cmd("OFTPPRM"). #var("id") is the final recipient. OFTPD discards its copy of the file and queues a NERP for the partner the file came from (#cmd("OFTP240P") follows). The reason in it is the one the partner gave, or 99 if there was none.], [Find out why the partner could not be reached or refused the file, from the #cmd("OFTP207P"), #cmd("OFTP212P") and #cmd("OFTP290P") lines before this one. The originator has to send the file again.])

#msg("OFTP243I", "PARTNER name ADDED BY user",
  [User #var("user") added the partner #var("name") to the partner table with #cmd("PARTNER ADD") of the utility or the ISPF dialog. OFTPD has stored the partner in the spool and in the table, and it is in use. The message does not show the passwords. #var("user") is #cmd("A CALLER WITHOUT USERID") if the caller has no user ID.],
  [None. Information only. To see the partner, use #cmd("OFTP PARTNER SHOW name").])

#msg("OFTP244I", "PARTNER name CHANGED BY user",
  [User #var("user") changed the partner #var("name") with #cmd("PARTNER CHANGE"), #cmd("PARTNER ENABLE") or #cmd("PARTNER DISABLE") of the utility, or with the ISPF dialog. OFTPD has stored the change in the spool and in the table. A partner that is in OFTPPTN is kept by command from its first change on, and the change is then a record of the spool that is used in place of the entry of OFTPPTN. The message is not written when the command changed nothing\; the answer is then #cmd("partner name unchanged"). The message does not show the passwords.],
  [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP245I", "PARTNER name DELETED BY user",
  [User #var("user") deleted the partner #var("name") with #cmd("PARTNER DELETE") of the utility or the ISPF dialog. OFTPD has deleted the record of the spool and taken the partner out of the table. If OFTPPTN has an entry of that name, OFTPD reads OFTPPTN again at once, so that the entry is used, and writes #cmd("OFTP016I").],
  [None. Information only.])

#msg("OFTP246P", "name: key SUBMITTED AS jobid: JOB pds(member) FOR dsn", [A file from partner #var("name") with spool key #var("key") was received as data set #var("dsn"), and the receive rule of the file has a #cmd("JOB="). OFTPD has read the member #var("pds(member)"), replaced its symbols and given it to the internal reader. #var("jobid") is the job number that JES gave it, or (JOB NUMBER UNKNOWN) if JES returned none. The message is written before #cmd("OFTP222P") for the same file. It says that JES took the job, not that the job ran or that the member was read to its end. The result is also in the transfer record, where #cmd("OFTP SHOW key") prints it.], [None. Information only. A job that fails when it runs fails in its own JES log.])

#msg("OFTP247W", "name: key NOT SUBMITTED, reason: JOB pds(member) FOR dsn", [A file from partner #var("name") with spool key #var("key") was received as data set #var("dsn"), and the receive rule of the file has a #cmd("JOB="), but the job was not submitted. The file is kept and acknowledged to the partner all the same. The reason is one of: MEMBER NOT FOUND OR NOT READABLE\; MEMBER NOT READABLE (the member could not be opened for the second pass)\; MEMBER IS EMPTY\; RECORD FORMAT NOT SUPPORTED (the record format is undefined or the record length is not positive)\; NO STORAGE\; RECORD PAST COLUMN 80\; LINE TOO LONG AFTER SUBSTITUTION\; ?VFILE NOT ALLOWED IN JCL (the virtual file name holds a character that is not a letter, a digit or one of the characters #cmd(". - _ @ # $"))\; INTERNAL READER OPEN FAILED (rc) and INTERNAL READER CLOSE FAILED (rc) with the return code of the call\; INTERNAL READER WRITE FAILED\; MEMBER CHANGED DURING READ (the second pass over the member found another number of records than the first, or could not read it to its end). RECORD PAST COLUMN 80, LINE TOO LONG AFTER SUBSTITUTION, ?VFILE NOT ALLOWED IN JCL and INTERNAL READER WRITE FAILED end in (RECORD n) with the number of the record. The reason stands first in the message, so that a long name cannot cut it off. A reason that is found only in the second pass can come after a part of the job has been written. That is the case for INTERNAL READER WRITE FAILED and MEMBER CHANGED DURING READ, for any other reason if the member was changed between the two passes, and possibly for INTERNAL READER CLOSE FAILED. That part may run although the message says NOT SUBMITTED (see @cfg-job-not). The reason is also in the transfer record, where #cmd("OFTP SHOW key") prints it.], [Correct the member or the rule, as the reason says. If the reason is INTERNAL READER WRITE FAILED, INTERNAL READER CLOSE FAILED or MEMBER CHANGED DURING READ, or if the member was edited just before, look in JES for a job that was started from the member and stop it if it is not wanted.])

#msg("OFTP248W", "name: dsn EXISTS AND SO DOES THE OLD VERSION dsn, DELETE dsn BY HAND", [At start OFTPD found a receive that a stop had interrupted. The rule had #cmd("REPLACE=Y"), and the stop came after the new file was renamed to the final name (the first name) and before the backup of the old one (the second name) was deleted. OFTPD deletes neither. The final name holds the new file, which is complete\; the backup holds the old version. The transfer is discarded from the spool, and as the partner did not get the receipt it sends the file again. #cmd("OFTP040P") follows.], [Compare the two data sets. Delete the backup when you do not need the old version. OFTPD does not delete it later.])

#msg("OFTP290P", "name: text", [The protocol engine reports a line of a session. #var("name") is the partner, or the caller's address while the partner is not known. The texts are in lower case, with names and numbers filled in. Normal work: "session with name: level n, buffer n, credit n, mode c" (with ", compressed" added when compression is on), "dsn sent, n records, n octets", "dsn received, n records, n octets" and "session ended normally". A file that is refused does not end the session: "dsn refused, SFNA nn retry c" (the partner refused a file OFTPD sent, or OFTPD refused one the partner offered, because the data set could not be created, see #cmd("OFTP220E")), "dsn refused, SFNA nn" (OFTPD refuses a file from its description, for example one that is encrypted, signed or compressed), "dsn not accepted, EFNA nn" (the partner refused the end of a file OFTPD sent, or OFTPD refused the end of a file it received because a count did not match or the file could not be written or closed, see #cmd("OFTP221E")), "dsn: cannot be described in an SFID (n)" and "dsn: response 'c' not possible at this level". Lines that end the session, and an #cmd("OFTP203P") line follows: "send failed", "connection lost (n)", "no answer within n s", "bad stream header xx", "stream length n outside 5..n", "session ended by us, ESID nn", "session ended by partner, ESID nn", "expected cmd, received command 'c'", "answer not compatible: level n buffer n credit n", "buffer n too small for level 5", "caller id refused, reason nn" (an incoming call refused, with the caller's Odette ID, as with reason 08 when the partner has all its sessions), "restart at n offered without restart", "dsn: read error, session ends", "DATA beyond the credit" and "malformed DATA buffer (n)". While tracing is on (#cmd("F OFTPD,TRACE ON")), each command gives "TRACE SEND cmd n: fields" or "TRACE RECV cmd n: fields", and the data buffers between two commands one line "TRACE SEND DATA n buffers, n octets" (or RECV). With the trace on for all partners, the first two lines of a session that the partner started, "TRACE SEND SSRM" and "TRACE RECV SSID", show the caller's address in place of the partner name, and for a caller that is refused or unknown every line does. After #cmd("F OFTPD,TRACE OFF") no trace line follows, also not from a session that is running. A session with three small files writes about 28 trace lines. If the log data set is full, OFTPD writes all further messages, the trace lines too, to the console (see #cmd("OFTP023E")). In these lines and in the lines about a caller or a received file, a character of an Odette ID or a file name from the partner that cannot be printed is shown as a dot.], [None for a normal run. For a line that ends a session, see the #cmd("OFTP203P") line after it and the #cmd("OFTP290W") line before it, if any. #cmd("F OFTPD,TRACE ON") traces all sessions and #cmd("F OFTPD,TRACE ON name") one partner. #cmd("F OFTPD,TRACE OFF") stops it.])

#msg("OFTP290W", "name: text", [The protocol engine reports a failed identity or password check. #var("name") is the partner, or the caller's address while the partner is not known. The session ends. The text is one of four. "unknown caller id" (with the caller's Odette ID): no enabled partner without #cmd("VIA=") in #cmd("OFTPPTN") has that Odette ID, or it called from another address than #cmd("ADDRESS=") (then #cmd("OFTP201W") is written first). The session ends with reason 03. "wrong password from name": the password the caller sent is not the #cmd("PASSWORD=") of its entry. An entry without #cmd("PASSWORD=") expects an empty password, so a caller that sends one is refused. Reason 04. "answered by id, not id": OFTPD called a partner, and the answer carries another Odette ID than the entry's #cmd("ODETTEID="). Reason 03. "partner password does not match": the answer to OFTPD's call carries a password that is not the entry's #cmd("PASSWORD="), which is an empty password when the entry has none. Reason 04. The texts are in lower case. A character of an Odette ID from the partner that cannot be printed is shown as a dot.], [Check the Odette ID and the passwords with the partner against its entry in #cmd("OFTPPTN"). After a change enter #cmd("F OFTPD,REFRESH"). Remember that #cmd("PASSWORD=") is what the partner sends to OFTPD and #cmd("OURPASSWORD=") is what OFTPD sends to the partner.])
